/*
Problem: Maximum Twin Sum of a Linked List (LC 2130)
Topic: Linked List / Two Pointers

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Store all linked list values in an
array.

Twin nodes are the first and last,
second and second-last, and so on.

Use two pointers from both ends of
the array to compute each twin sum
and keep track of the maximum.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Traverse the linked list and store
   all node values in a vector.

2. Initialize:

   left = 0

   right = last index

3. While left < right:

   • Compute twin sum.

   • Update the maximum sum.

   • Move both pointers inward.

4. Return the maximum twin sum.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

Twin pairs:

(1,4) = 5

(2,3) = 5

Answer:

5

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

4 → 2 → 2 → 3

Twin pairs:

(4,3) = 7

(2,2) = 4

Answer:

7

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The vector stores nodes in their
original order.

Using two pointers from both ends
naturally forms all twin pairs.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

One traversal to build the vector and
one traversal to compute twin sums.

Space : O(n)

Extra vector stores all node values.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Linked List / Two Pointers
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int pairSum(ListNode* head) {

        vector<int> values;

        // Store linked list values
        ListNode* temp = head;

        while (temp != NULL) {

            values.push_back(temp->val);

            temp = temp->next;
        }

        int left = 0;
        int right = values.size() - 1;

        int maxSum = 0;

        // Compute twin sums
        while (left < right) {

            int sum =
                values[left] + values[right];

            maxSum = max(maxSum, sum);

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return maxSum;
    }
};
